  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 234 PM EDT Tue Oct 27 2026\n\nOnce again looking at the potential for some fog development\novernight tonight with a light easterly wind that may allow for\nmoisture to advect westward into northwestern OH and\nnortheastern IN locations. Patchy to areas of dense fog will be\npossible in these locations. Subsequent shifts will be\nmonitoring for this occurrence as there is still some \nuncertainty due to winds as if they are lighter than expected \nnot too much advection will occur but if winds slightly stronger\nthan more moisture would work it's way into the area increasing\nthe chances and locations that may see fog development tonight.\n\n\nMuch above normal temperatures look to continue through Thursday\nbut a cool frontal boundary associated with a weak disturbance will\narrive on Thursday and begin to push a more zonal synoptic flow\naloft over the region which will also be accompanied by \nincreasing clouds. Highs into the low to mid 70s Wednesday and \nThursday then highs mainly in the 60s expected each day into \nnext week with the increase in cloud cover. A few weak \ndisturbances ripple through the westerly flow bringing chances \nfor light rain showers on Halloween and again on Sunday. On \nHalloween, the better chances will be over the northwestern \nportions of the area but amounts look fairly light so likely not\ntoo impactful to any festivities. On Sunday, most of the area\nwill have a chance for some light showers. \n\nSunday into next week a fairly complicated synoptic pattern\nlooks to converge across the central CONUS with both a southern\ncirculation/trof and a northern trof and circulation over\ncentral southern Canada. This looks to bring a much active\npattern to the area. Latest guidance has a more temporarily \ncutoff southern feature due to the lack of phasing but systems \nare expected to potentially phase and this could bring a much \ncolder airmass into the Great Lakes region later next week. Will\nbe watching to see how this evolves over the next few days as\nthis looks to be a potentially impactful system.",
